Title:
dotnet-gargoyle — Gargoyle-style memory scanning evasion for .NET

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This repository provides a proof-of-concept “spiritual .NET equivalent” of the Gargoyle technique for evading in-memory scanning. It focuses on making malicious or sensitive .NET code harder to detect by memory scanners by controlling when and how code/data is present in readable memory. The solution includes multiple projects (managed and native) suggesting a demo setup with a .NET assembly, a native host/app, and helper components to illustrate the evasion workflow. It is primarily useful for red teamers, offensive security researchers, and malware developers studying .NET tradecraft and defensive gaps in memory scanning. It’s interesting because it adapts a known evasion concept to the .NET runtime context, where managed execution, JIT, and runtime structures change the scanning/detection surface.

Technical Focus:
- Memory scanning evasion (Gargoyle-inspired)
- .NET runtime/managed code execution considerations (JIT/assemblies)
- Native + managed interop (mixed-mode demo architecture)
- Timing-based techniques (timer enumeration component)
- In-memory payload/assembly handling and exposure reduction

Use Cases:
- Build a lab PoC to evaluate EDR/AV memory scanning coverage against managed payloads
- Research .NET-specific adaptations of classic in-memory evasion techniques
- Develop and test detection ideas for transient/deferred in-memory artifacts in .NET processes
- Demonstrate offensive tradecraft concepts in training or internal red team R&D

Keywords:
.NET, CLR, managed code, memory scanning, evasion, Gargoyle, in-memory payload, JIT compilation, assembly loading, native interop, P/Invoke, Windows process memory, EDR bypass research, timers, runtime artifacts, Countercept, WithSecureLabs